{
  "id": "ep094",
  "duration": 57.698,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.721,
      "text": "A new asteroid held a terrifying 3.1 percent chance of hitting Earth.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Asteroid 2024 YR4 Flyby Still (noirlab2514c).jpg",
        "credit": "NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/R. Proctor",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asteroid_2024_YR4_Flyby_Still_(noirlab2514c).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 1.967,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 1.967,
          "end": 4.733,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.721,
      "end": 15.191,
      "text": "On December 27, 2024, the ATLAS telescope in Chile spotted asteroid 2024 YR4 sneaking past a solar blind spot.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.733,
          "end": 15.2,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 15.191,
      "end": 22.916,
      "text": "The city-killer reached Level 3 on the Torino Scale, the highest impact probability ever recorded for its size.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 15.2,
          "end": 22.9,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 22.916,
      "end": 30.261,
      "text": "Roughly 53 to 67 meters wide, it packed the explosive punch of 8 megatons of TNT.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "2024 YR4 size comparison.png",
        "credit": "Sinucep",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2024_YR4_size_comparison.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 22.9,
          "end": 30.267,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.261,
      "end": 36.206,
      "text": "Its potential impact corridor stretched across parts of South America, India, and Nigeria.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "2024 YR4 size comparison.png",
        "credit": "Sinucep",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2024_YR4_size_comparison.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.267,
          "end": 36.2,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 36.206,
      "end": 43.376,
      "text": "Then, James Webb Space Telescope observations ruled out Earth, leaving a 4 percent chance of hitting the Moon.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "2024 YR4 JWST NIRCam F150W 2025-03-08.png",
        "credit": "A. S. Rivkin et al./NASA/ESA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2024_YR4_JWST_NIRCam_F150W_2025-03-08.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 36.2,
          "end": 43.367,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 43.376,
      "end": 51.546,
      "text": "Refined 2026 data finally cleared the asteroid entirely, ending the most threatening impact scare in history.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Moon LSETM (35970428073).jpg",
        "credit": "Giuseppe Donatiello",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moon_LSETM_(35970428073).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 43.367,
          "end": 46.367,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 46.367,
          "end": 51.533,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 51.546,
      "end": 57.348,
      "text": "That is how a new space rock briefly held a terrifying 3.1 percent chance of hitting Earth.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Asteroid 2024 YR4 Flyby Still (noirlab2514c).jpg",
        "credit": "NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/R. Proctor",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asteroid_2024_YR4_Flyby_Still_(noirlab2514c).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 51.533,
